body:
how are you starting `nvim` ?

does this patch help?

```diff
diff --git a/src/nvim/main.c b/src/nvim/main.c
index 8ff873e1279f..701201fef46c 100644
--- a/src/nvim/main.c
+++ b/src/nvim/main.c
@@ -354,6 +354,7 @@ int main(int argc, char **argv)
     if (use_remote_ui) {
       remote_ui_wait_for_attach();
     } else {
+      input_stop();  // Stop reading input, let the UI take over.
       ui_builtin_start();
     }
     TIME_MSG("done waiting for UI");
```

Here is patch against the commit that breaks C-h that makes it work again.  I don't know this code but wanted to narrow it down a bit more to what part of the patch broke it.
```diff --git a/src/nvim/main.c b/src/nvim/main.c
index 059b8762e..a919bd1f1 100644
--- a/src/nvim/main.c
+++ b/src/nvim/main.c
@@ -319,10 +319,15 @@ int main(int argc, char **argv)
   //
   // Read ex-commands if invoked with "-es".
   //
+    bool reading_tty = !headless_mode
+                            && !embedded_mode
+                            && !silent_mode
+                            && (params.input_isatty || params.output_isatty
+                                || params.err_isatty);
   bool reading_excmds = !params.input_isatty
                         && silent_mode
                         && exmode_active == EXMODE_NORMAL;
-  if (reading_excmds) {
+  if (reading_tty || reading_excmds) {
     int fd = STDIN_FILENO;
     input_start(fd);
   }
```

@dcardamo Thanks! I tried to simulate the same behavior on master:
```diff
diff --git a/src/nvim/main.c b/src/nvim/main.c
index 8ff873e12..5dee129d7 100644
--- a/src/nvim/main.c
+++ b/src/nvim/main.c
@@ -354,6 +354,8 @@ int main(int argc, char **argv)
     if (use_remote_ui) {
       remote_ui_wait_for_attach();
     } else {
+      input_start(STDIN_FILENO);
+      input_stop();
       ui_builtin_start();
     }
     TIME_MSG("done waiting for UI");
```

And it works, but I don't understand why it works. All it does is to open and close a reading stream, before it opens the reading stream on the TUI thread (using the same `rstream.c` code on both threads).
